WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.360
I think most people
would be surprised

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.160
at how much information
they unknowingly give away online

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.840
and how that can leave them
vulnerable when people like me

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.120
come along and collect
all of that information.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.680
It's incredibly easy to track anyone
with any modern technology now.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.280
It's incredibly difficult for anyone
to try and avoid detection.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.600
When I went on the run
back in the 1990s

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.600
there was still, just about,
a chance to do it

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.600
because it was
very resource intensive

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.480
for either spies or corporations to
track people who went on the run.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.960
I'm Scott Helme, I'm a cybersecurity
researcher and an ethical hacker.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.440
I'm Annie Machon, I'm a former
intelligence officer with MI5,

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.280
the UK domestic security service.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.960
So I think many people
would be very surprised

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.120
about what we can find out
about them online.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.680
I met someone recently
where we demonstrated this

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.560
and with just their name and knowing
the general area where they lived

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.360
we were able to narrow down
their social media profile.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.160
We have things like the births,
marriages and deaths register

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.000
in the UK where we can
look up your marriage certificate

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.040
or your birth certificate.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.840
From there we can see,
parents and spouses

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.960
and we can start to spread out this
net of information

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.960
that we can gather about you
from very simple resources.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.000
Social media over the last decade
has become the spies dream.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.840
Back in the 1990s, my job
as an intelligence officer

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.720
was to build up profiles
and investigate targets

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.720
and that could take weeks; trying to
get a shape of someone's life.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.920
Their contacts, where they worked,
their relationships,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.560
their views, their activities,
their hobbies, everything.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.840
Now of course with social media,

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.000
we just offer it all up for free,
voluntarily.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.040
I think privacy has changed so
dramatically in the last 10 years

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.720
if we go back 10 years
it would have been very difficult

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.160
to gather information about someone,
very analogue, very offline.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.520
We may have to collect
pieces of paper or documents

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.560
and now in the digital world it's

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.800
a click of a finger
and a search on the internet

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.000
and we can have a lot of information
about someone.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.680
Social is really easy where you can
pick up Twitter, send a tweet

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.320
that my internet has stopped working
so I might tweet my internet company

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.320
or more recently,
my water supply stops working

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.360
and the water company
will have status updates

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.240
and people will comment on them and
we now know that all of these people

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.760
are users of this company's services

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.680
so if I wanted to impersonate them
I could call one of those individuals

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.400
if I had their number and say, "Hey,
Sarah, I'm from the water company.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.680
Just regarding your complaint, could
we go through account security,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.320
could you confirm
your postcode for me please?"

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.840
And I'm then extracting
more information

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.120
using the original tweet as the
leverage to get you to trust me.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.360
It's interesting to ask,
"Who might be spying on us?"

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.520
I mean, obviously for someone who
worked in the intelligence agencies

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.920
I think of espionage and spying
and surveillance in a particular way

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.560
where you are
given targets to investigate.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.640
First of all, yes it might be the law
enforcement intelligence agencies,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.200
depending on
what you're getting up to.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.680
It could be political activism,
as simple as that.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.680
Two, it will definitely be the big
social media corporations

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:05.800
because we are their product

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.280
and the question is then
to realistically say,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.480
"OK, what might be
the threat to me personally?

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.880
How can I best protect myself from
those threats?"

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.080
You don’t have to be on the run
from the government or MI5.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.800
You might just want a bit of privacy.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.280
Smart phones are little
spy phones in your pocket.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.280
Turn off your smartphone;
ideally leave it at home.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.760
Another really good thing for people
to look at is the privacy settings

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.640
on applications on their smartphones.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.000
So maybe you don't want
an application

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.440
to be able to look
through your photographs.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.480
You can go into the settings on your
phone and disable these things.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.800
So go into your device's settings,

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.080
have a look through those
and just think,

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.960
"You know, this app doesn't
need my microphone.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.320
So I'm just gonna turn that off,
make sure it can't listen to me.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.960
It doesn't need my location, I'm just
gonna turn that off as well."

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.080
And just start to take
a little bit more control

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.080
over all of the information
that you're sharing.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.640
One thing you can do of course is to
invest in older technology.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.480
Get an old burner phone

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.600
that you might just use for
phone calls and that's it.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.600
Use an old laptop and then
a whole suite of privacy software

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.080
on that laptop as well.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:07.840
Another really great way of
protecting yourself

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.000
if you're travelling a lot
using public WIFI

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.560
at an airport or a train station,

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.880
they commonly ask for your first name
and last name in order to log in

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:16.040
what you can do is

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.440
give them a different first name
and last name to log in

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.120
and you'll still be able to use the
WIFI network afterwards.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.640
This may sound a little paranoid but
when I was in Berlin a few years ago

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.440
at the height of
the Snowden disclosures

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.560
if you went to parties
you were expected to

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.360
put your mobile phones
in a tin, a biscuit tin

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.880
which then went into the fridge to
stop signals going in and coming out

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.720
to stop the phone snooping on you.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.760
I think it's really important that
people make conscious decisions

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.440
about the information that they share

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.760
because often
when we share this information

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.640
you can never guarantee
to be able to take that back.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.680
If I send a tweet to a company

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.000
and I want to make a complaint
about their service

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.120
I may be able to
delete that tweet later

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.520
but everyone's already seen that
and knows that I had that interaction

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.400
so making this conscious decision
before I take those actions

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.160
would help me prevent
leaking that information later.

